---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Plugin & skills — TokenMonitor"
  description="Install the tokenmonitor MCP plugin for Claude, Codex or Antigravity — from the command line, or the desktop app for Claude and Codex — plus the four skills and 17 MCP tools it adds."
  canonical="https://tokenmonitor.dev/plugin.html"
  page="plugin"
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Plugin · skills · tools</p>
      <h1>One MCP plugin.<br>Three AI ecosystems.</h1>
      <div class="rule"></div>
      <p class="lead">The <code class="inline-code">tokenmonitor</code> plugin ships through Fractal Manifold's MCP marketplace. Claude, Codex and Antigravity each install it from the command line — and for Claude and Codex, from the desktop app too. Pick your ecosystem below, then the route you prefer, and jump to the <a href="#skills" style="color:var(--color-gold-light)">skills &amp; tools reference</a> when you're set up.</p>

      <div class="cli-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Choose your AI ecosystem">
        <button type="button" id="clitab-claude" class="cli-tab claude active" data-cli="claude"
                role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="clipanel-claude" tabindex="0">
          <span class="swatch claude"></span>
          <span class="label">Claude Code</span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" id="clitab-codex" class="cli-tab codex" data-cli="codex"
                role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="clipanel-codex" tabindex="-1">
          <span class="swatch codex"></span>
          <span class="label">Codex CLI</span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" id="clitab-antigravity" class="cli-tab antigravity" data-cli="antigravity"
                role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="clipanel-antigravity" tabindex="-1">
          <span class="swatch antigravity"></span>
          <span class="label">Antigravity CLI</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Claude Code panel ───────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="cli-panel active" id="clipanel-claude" data-panel="claude"
           role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="clitab-claude" tabindex="0">
        <div class="panel-head">
          <h2>Claude Code</h2>
          <span class="marketplace">via <a href="https://github.com/fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace</a></span>
        </div>

        <!-- Route switcher: terminal vs desktop app -->
        <div class="route-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="How to install on Claude">
          <button type="button" id="route-claude-terminal" class="route-tab active" data-route="terminal"
                  role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="routepanel-claude-terminal" tabindex="0">
            <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 17l6-5-6-5"/><path d="M12 19h8"/></svg>
            Terminal
          </button>
          <button type="button" id="route-claude-desktop" class="route-tab" data-route="desktop"
                  role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="routepanel-claude-desktop" tabindex="-1">
            <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4"/></svg>
            Desktop app
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="route-panel active" id="routepanel-claude-terminal" data-route-panel="terminal"
             role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="route-claude-terminal" tabindex="0">
          <p class="route-note">Claude Code natively supports MCP marketplaces. Two commands — first you add the marketplace, then you install the plugin from it.</p>

          <h4>Install</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">&gt;</span> /plugin marketplace add fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace
<span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">&gt;</span> /plugin install tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</div>

          <h4>What it adds</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Skills (you invoke these)</span>
/tokenmonitor:configure
/tokenmonitor:theme <span class="accent" style="color:var(--color-purple-light)">&lt;day|night|auto&gt;</span>
/tokenmonitor:settings <span class="accent" style="color:var(--color-purple-light)">&lt;change&gt;…</span>
/tokenmonitor:firmware

<span class="cmt"># Tools the model calls as needed — the diagnostic four,</span>
<span class="cmt"># plus control-plane tools the skills drive for you</span>
tokenmonitor_status
tokenmonitor_health
tokenmonitor_recent_logs
tokenmonitor_provision_hint</div>

          <h4>Where it lives</h4>
          <p class="route-fine">Marketplace entries land in <code class="inline-code">~/.claude/plugins/</code>. Uninstall any time with <code class="inline-code">/plugin uninstall tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="route-panel" id="routepanel-claude-desktop" data-route-panel="desktop"
             role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="route-claude-desktop" tabindex="0">
          <p class="route-note">Prefer clicking to typing? The Claude desktop app installs the very same plugin from its Settings panel — same skills, same tools, no terminal needed.</p>
          <ol class="gui-steps">
            <li>Open <strong>Settings → Plugins</strong>, then click <strong>Add ▾</strong> in the top-right and choose <strong>Add marketplace</strong>.</li>
            <li>Enter the marketplace repo <code class="inline-code">fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace</code> and confirm.</li>
            <li>Back in the plugin list, find <strong>Tokenmonitor</strong> by <em>fractal-manifold</em> and click <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
          </ol>
          <figure class="shot">
            <img src="/assets/claude-desktop-add-marketplace.png"
                 alt="Claude desktop Settings → Plugins with the Add menu open, showing the Add marketplace option"
                 width="720" height="232" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Claude desktop → Settings → Plugins → <strong>Add ▾</strong> → <strong>Add marketplace</strong>.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Codex CLI panel ─────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="cli-panel" id="clipanel-codex" data-panel="codex"
           role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="clitab-codex" tabindex="0">
        <div class="panel-head">
          <h2>Codex CLI</h2>
          <span class="marketplace">via <a href="https://github.com/fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace</a></span>
        </div>

        <!-- Route switcher: terminal vs desktop app -->
        <div class="route-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="How to install on Codex">
          <button type="button" id="route-codex-terminal" class="route-tab active" data-route="terminal"
                  role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="routepanel-codex-terminal" tabindex="0">
            <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 17l6-5-6-5"/><path d="M12 19h8"/></svg>
            Terminal
          </button>
          <button type="button" id="route-codex-desktop" class="route-tab" data-route="desktop"
                  role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="routepanel-codex-desktop" tabindex="-1">
            <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4"/></svg>
            Desktop app
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="route-panel active" id="routepanel-codex-terminal" data-route-panel="terminal"
             role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="route-codex-terminal" tabindex="0">
          <p class="route-note">Codex CLI reads the same marketplace Claude Code does. Two shell commands — add the marketplace, then install the plugin from it. Nothing else to install first: the plugin bundles its own server.</p>

          <h4>Install</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> codex plugin marketplace add fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace
<span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> codex plugin add tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</div>
          <p class="route-fine" style="margin-top: -0.4rem;">Skills and MCP servers are read when a thread starts, so <strong>open a new Codex thread</strong> afterwards. Confirm with <code class="inline-code">codex plugin list</code> — <code class="inline-code">tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</code> should read <em>installed, enabled</em>.</p>

          <h4>What it adds</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Skills (you invoke these) — the CLI gets all four</span>
/tokenmonitor:configure
/tokenmonitor:theme <span class="accent" style="color:var(--color-purple-light)">&lt;day|night|auto&gt;</span>
/tokenmonitor:settings <span class="accent" style="color:var(--color-purple-light)">&lt;change&gt;…</span>
/tokenmonitor:firmware

<span class="cmt"># Tools Codex calls as needed — the diagnostic four,</span>
<span class="cmt"># plus control-plane tools the skills drive for you</span>
tokenmonitor_status
tokenmonitor_health
tokenmonitor_recent_logs
tokenmonitor_provision_hint</div>

          <h4>Where it lives</h4>
          <p class="route-fine">The plugin is cached per-user under <code class="inline-code">~/.codex/plugins/cache/fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace/tokenmonitor/&lt;version&gt;/</code>. Installing writes the marketplace source and an <code class="inline-code">enabled</code> flag to <code class="inline-code">~/.codex/config.toml</code> — you never hand-author an <code class="inline-code">[mcp_servers]</code> block.</p>

          <h4>Update &amp; remove</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># updating takes both steps — refreshing the marketplace</span>
<span class="cmt"># snapshot alone leaves the cached plugin where it was</span>
<span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> codex plugin marketplace upgrade fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace
<span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> codex plugin add tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace

<span class="cmt"># uninstall</span>
<span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> codex plugin remove tokenmonitor@fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</div>
          <p class="route-fine" style="margin-top: -0.4rem;">Either way, start a fresh thread — a running one keeps whatever it loaded at start.</p>

          <div class="route-warn">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
            <div><strong>Don't register it by hand.</strong> A <code class="inline-code">codex mcp add</code> entry pointed at a <code class="inline-code">tokenmonitor-mcp</code> binary gets you neither the four skills nor the plugin manifest's 60-second first-start allowance — which the server needs the one time it resolves its runtime. Go through the marketplace.</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="route-panel" id="routepanel-codex-desktop" data-route-panel="desktop"
             role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="route-codex-desktop" tabindex="0">
          <p class="route-note">The Codex desktop app browses the same marketplace. Same plugin, same skills and tools — clicked instead of typed.</p>
          <ol class="gui-steps">
            <li>Open <strong>Plugins</strong>, click <strong>Create ▾</strong> in the top-right and choose <strong>Add marketplace</strong>.</li>
            <li>Point it at the marketplace repo <code class="inline-code">fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace</code>.</li>
            <li>The <code class="inline-code">fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</code> section appears — install the <strong>TokenMonitor</strong> plugin (&ldquo;Control and diagnose TokenMonitor&hellip;&rdquo;).</li>
          </ol>
          <figure class="shot shot-wide">
            <img src="/assets/codex-desktop-add-marketplace.png"
                 alt="Codex desktop Plugins page with the Create menu open, showing the Add marketplace option"
                 width="1325" height="365" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Codex desktop → Plugins → <strong>Create ▾</strong> → <strong>Add marketplace</strong>.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="shot">
            <img src="/assets/codex-desktop-tokenmonitor-installed.png"
                 alt="Codex desktop Plugins page showing the fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace section with the TokenMonitor plugin"
                 width="932" height="752" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>The <code class="inline-code">fractalmanifold-mcp-marketplace</code> section, with the TokenMonitor plugin ready to install.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Antigravity CLI panel ───────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="cli-panel" id="clipanel-antigravity" data-panel="antigravity"
           role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="clitab-antigravity" tabindex="0">
        <div class="panel-head">
          <h2>Antigravity CLI</h2>
          <span class="marketplace">via <a href="https://github.com/fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace</a></span>
        </div>

        <!-- Antigravity ships no desktop app, so this panel has one route only —
             deliberately no sub-tab bar rather than a disabled second segment. -->
        <div class="route-solo">
          <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 17l6-5-6-5"/><path d="M12 19h8"/></svg>
          Terminal only
        </div>

        <p class="route-note">Antigravity — the <code class="inline-code">agy</code> CLI, Google's successor to the Gemini CLI — installs plugins straight from a GitHub subpath. One command; the plugin is self-contained (it bundles its own MCP server), so there's no separate broker install to wire up. (It still runs the Gemini-family models, e.g. <code class="inline-code">gemini-3.5-flash</code> and <code class="inline-code">gemini-3.1-pro</code>.)</p>

        <h4>Install</h4>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt" style="color:var(--color-gold)">$</span> agy plugin install https://github.com/fractal-manifold/mcp-marketplace/plugins/tokenmonitor</div>
        <p class="route-fine" style="margin-top: -0.4rem;">Note the subcommand is <code class="inline-code">plugin</code> (not <code class="inline-code">extensions</code>), and the target is the full <code class="inline-code">https://github.com/…</code> URL — a bare <code class="inline-code">owner/repo</code> shorthand is rejected.</p>

        <h4>Where it lands</h4>
        <span class="file-tag">~/.gemini/config/plugins/tokenmonitor/gemini-extension.json</span>
<div class="code-block">&#123;
  <span class="str">"name"</span>:    <span class="str">"tokenmonitor"</span>,
  <span class="str">"version"</span>: <span class="str">"1.0.3"</span>,
  <span class="str">"mcpServers"</span>: &#123;
    <span class="str">"tokenmonitor"</span>: &#123;
      <span class="str">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"sh"</span>,
      <span class="str">"args"</span>:    [<span class="str">"$&#123;extensionPath&#125;/server/tokenmonitor-mcp"</span>]
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</div>
        <p class="route-fine" style="margin-top: -0.4rem;">The install copies the whole plugin, and the manifest above registers the bundled <code class="inline-code">server/</code> launcher — so the MCP tools are available. The manifest declares no skills or hooks: the session-start hook does not run under Antigravity, and whether the <code class="inline-code">/tokenmonitor:…</code> skills appear as commands depends on the client — if they don't, ask in plain language and the model drives the same tools. Start a fresh <code class="inline-code">agy</code> session to load it; remove it any time with <code class="inline-code">agy plugin uninstall tokenmonitor</code>.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Verify (common to all three) ────────────────────── -->
      <div class="verify">
        <div class="head">
          <div class="check">
            <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5L20 7"/></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Verify the plugin is loaded</h3>
            <p>Same prompt works in any of the three apps. It hits <code class="inline-code">tokenmonitor_status</code> under the hood.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="chat-bubble user">
          What's the status of my TokenMonitor broker?
        </div>
        <div class="chat-bubble cli">
          The broker is running with role <code>leader</code> on <code>localhost:8765</code>. It last served <code>tmon-3c7c9a02</code> a few seconds ago and has handled <strong style="color: var(--color-gold-light);">2,847 requests</strong> since it started 4&nbsp;hours ago. Health: <strong style="color: var(--color-success);">PASS</strong>.
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--fg-3);">If the answer comes back with a different broker role, an empty request count, or the app says it can't find the tool — head to the <a href="/faq.html" style="color: var(--color-gold-light);">troubleshooting page</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <!-- ── Skills & tools reference ────────────────────────── -->
      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <div class="part-head" id="skills">
        <p class="eyebrow">Skills &amp; MCP tools</p>
        <h2>Four skills. Seventeen tools.</h2>
        <div class="rule"></div>
        <p class="lead">Skills are explicit commands you invoke; tools are functions the model can call on your behalf. In Claude Code and Codex both are loaded the moment the <code class="inline-code">tokenmonitor</code> plugin is installed — nothing else to wire up. Every client gets the tools; skill support depends on the client.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Skills (pulled from SKILL.md frontmatter) ────────── -->
      <div class="skills-grid">
        <article class="skill-card">
          <div class="skill-name">/tokenmonitor:configure</div>
          <h3>Pair or reconfigure a device</h3>
          <p class="desc">Provision a new device, or reconfigure an existing one, over the LAN or a USB cable. On the LAN it discovers the device via mDNS and prompts for the 6-digit pairing code; over USB no code is needed and it can also change Wi-Fi without a factory reset. A first pairing generates a key and registers the device locally; a device the broker already knows keeps its key, and a Wi-Fi-only or settings-only change leaves the pairing untouched. On firmware 1.0.0 and newer the device finds its broker by mDNS; for older units the skill pushes the broker's address along with the key.</p>

          <h4>When to use</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="b"></span>New device showing "Waiting for setup" / pairing code on screen</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Changing the Wi-Fi network of a device you already set up</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Reaching a device on a guest network or VLAN, over the cable</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Re-establishing trust after a device factory reset</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Syntax</h4>
          <div class="syntax"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:configure</span></div>

          <h4>What it does, step by step</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Scans <code class="inline-code">_tmon._tcp.local</code> for advertised devices (or the serial ports, for USB)</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Prompts you for the pairing code on the device (LAN only)</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Generates a random per-device key (or hashes a passphrase you set)</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Sends the key (and any settings) to the device — over the LAN, or down the USB cable</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Writes the device entry under <code class="inline-code">~/.config/tokenmonitor/devices/</code></li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="skill-card">
          <div class="skill-name">/tokenmonitor:theme</div>
          <h3>Switch themes remotely</h3>
          <p class="desc">Flip a device between Day, Night or Auto themes from the comfort of your CLI. Auto follows sunrise/sunset for the device's configured city with a ±90 s hysteresis.</p>

          <h4>When to use</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Force Day mode for a screenshot</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Force Night mode when you're working late and the sensor lags</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Reset to Auto after manual overrides</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Syntax</h4>
          <div class="syntax"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:theme</span> <span class="opt">&lt;day | night | auto&gt;</span> [<span class="opt">--device</span> <span class="arg">&lt;device_id&gt;</span>]</div>

          <h4>Examples</h4>
          <div class="syntax" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:theme</span> auto</div>
          <div class="syntax" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:theme</span> night <span class="opt">--device</span> tmon-3c7c9a02</div>
          <div class="syntax"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:theme</span> day <span class="opt">--device</span> kitchen</div>
        </article>

        <article class="skill-card">
          <div class="skill-name">/tokenmonitor:settings</div>
          <h3>Reconfigure a provisioned device</h3>
          <p class="desc">Push runtime settings to an already-paired device via the control plane: city, day / night brightness, alert volume, which providers are enabled, auto-rotation, theme, pet, custom panel, passphrase rotation. The change applies on the device's next poll — about 10 seconds — with automatic rollback if it can't be promoted.</p>

          <h4>When to use</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Set the city to Madrid"</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Lower night brightness to 15"</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Mute alerts" / "set volume to 0"</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Disable Codex on the TokenMonitor"</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Rotate providers every 60 s"</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>"Rotate the broker passphrase"</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Syntax</h4>
          <div class="syntax"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:settings</span> <span class="opt">&lt;natural-language change&gt;</span> [<span class="opt">--device</span> <span class="arg">&lt;device_id&gt;</span>]</div>

          <h4>Out of scope</h4>
          <p class="desc" style="font-size:0.88rem">This skill will not change WiFi blind. Ask it to and it hands you to <code class="inline-code">/tokenmonitor:configure</code>, which does it properly: over a <strong>USB cable</strong> (no factory reset, and it keeps the pairing key), or over the LAN when the device already remembers the network you are naming. Note the cable is only listening while the device is in <strong>pairing mode</strong> — on a device that is already online, hold <strong>BOOT</strong> for 3–10 seconds and let go first; it reboots into pairing mode and then the USB route works. A device only ever auto-joins networks it has a password for, so an open network has to be chosen over the cable or on the device's own screen. Display and provider tweaks are also available in the on-device Settings panel (tap the gear on the dashboard). On firmware 1.0.1 and newer there is no broker address to change either: the device finds its broker by mDNS, so moving it to another machine means registering the same device ID and key on that machine's broker. Older units pin the address — the skill can re-point one to the same broker at a new address, and moving one to a different broker means re-pairing it with <code class="inline-code">/tokenmonitor:configure</code>.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="skill-card">
          <div class="skill-name">/tokenmonitor:firmware</div>
          <h3>Stage a signed firmware update</h3>
          <p class="desc">Build signed firmware from the repo (<code class="inline-code">make build-prod</code>) and stage it as an over-the-air update across your LAN, through the broker — dev channel first, as a canary on test units, then stable. Each image is an Ed25519-signed manifest the device verifies before the boot slot switches — with automatic rollback if the new build doesn't check in healthy, and an anti-rollback floor against downgrades.</p>

          <h4>When to use</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="b"></span>You built a new firmware version and want it on a device</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>You want to move a device onto the dev / canary channel</li>
            <li><span class="b"></span>Re-applying after an automatic rollback</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Syntax</h4>
          <div class="syntax"><span class="arg">/tokenmonitor:firmware</span> [<span class="opt">--device</span> <span class="arg">&lt;device_id&gt;</span>]</div>

          <h4>Safe by design</h4>
          <p class="desc" style="font-size:0.88rem">Updates only ever flow over your own LAN, and the device installs nothing it can't cryptographically verify. If a build won't boot cleanly, it reverts itself.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <!-- ── 4 of the 17 MCP tools (table-style) ────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="tools-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">MCP tools</p>
        <h2>What the model can call directly.</h2>
        <div class="rule"></div>
        <p class="lead" style="max-width: 64ch;">These are the four of the 17 you'll reach for directly — your AI agent calls them in response to natural-language questions, no slash-skill needed. The skills drive a larger set of control-plane tools (device registry, discovery, LAN and USB provisioning, Wi-Fi changes, firmware publish / revert / update checks) behind the scenes.</p>

        <div class="tools-grid">
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-name">tokenmonitor_status</div>
            <h4>Runtime, role + request count</h4>
            <p>Returns the broker's runtime, its role (<code class="inline-code">leader</code> or <code class="inline-code">follower</code>), when it last served the device, and the total requests handled since startup.</p>
            <div class="returns">
              <span>runtime</span><span>role: leader|follower</span><span>last_request_at</span><span>requests_total</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-name">tokenmonitor_health</div>
            <h4>PASS / FAIL diagnostic per check</h4>
            <p>Runs a PASS/FAIL check across your provider credentials (present, parseable, not expired), a signed self-ping to the broker, and recently observed device traffic.</p>
            <div class="returns">
              <span>ok</span><span>credentials</span><span>self_ping</span><span>observed_traffic</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-name">tokenmonitor_recent_logs</div>
            <h4>Tail the broker log</h4>
            <p>Returns the last N lines (default 50) of the shared broker daemon's log. Useful when a device shows stale data and you want to see whether the broker even received the poll.</p>
            <div class="returns">
              <span>limit: string</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-name">tokenmonitor_provision_hint</div>
            <h4>Suggests laptop LAN URLs</h4>
            <p>Lists the URLs your broker is reachable on (your laptop's LAN interfaces plus the broker port). Mostly a diagnostic: on firmware 1.0.0 and newer the device finds its broker by mDNS. Older units need the address pushed at pairing: over the LAN the tool sends this host's address on the route to the device, and over USB it uses this list when it has exactly one URL (otherwise it asks).</p>
            <div class="returns">
              <span>port</span><span>bind</span><span>hosts[]</span><span>urls[]</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

